  1720	# npm-publish.yml at tag cut" was written in the INTRODUCTION, not in a row.
  1721	#
  1722	# Rule: a claim that a mechanism runs may live in exactly two places — a
  1723	# Contract table row (machine-checked by
  1724	# test_integrity_contract_rows_name_a_live_step) or §Not yet automated (tied
  1725	# to the table's un-enforced rows by
  1726	# test_integrity_unenforced_rows_are_restated_where_the_reader_looks).
  1727	# Anywhere else it is answerable to nothing, and is a red.
  1728	#
  1729	# PREDICATE CHOICE (the honest part). This is a closed VOCABULARY list with
  1730	# NO negation exemption, and both halves were chosen from evidence, not taste:
  1731	#
  1732	#   * An earlier draft tolerated a phrase when a negation appeared within ~120
  1733	#     chars. Probing the real file showed that exemption firing by ACCIDENT —
  1734	#     "MUST satisfy" was excused by a "not" belonging to the next paragraph,
  1735	#     and "fail-closes" by the "not" inside "does not verify". An exemption
  1736	#     that triggers on unrelated words is not an exemption, it is a hole. So
  1737	#     the prose was cured instead (PLAN-177 W0.3 delta: the intro now points
  1738	#     at the table rather than enumerating mechanisms, and the tag-signing
  1739	#     paragraph cites SECURITY.md as the authority instead of restating the
  1740	#     gate), and the rule became absolute. Zero occurrences today: the sweep
  1741	#     needs no exemption, so it has none.
  1742	#   * The list is deliberately NARROW: phrases that assert a mechanism is in
  1743	#     force. Bare "verify" is absent on purpose — the file legitimately tells
  1744	#     a reader to `Verify locally with gpg --import ...`, an instruction, not
  1745	#     a claim about the pipeline. "verifies"/"is verified" ARE listed: those
  1746	#     are the file speaking about what the pipeline does.
  1747	#     A broad list that has to be switched off is worth less than a narrow
  1748	#     one that stays on.
  1749	#
  1750	# Matching is on word boundaries after normalization. Substring matching was
  1751	# tried first and reported "asserted by" inside "asserted byte-for-byte" — the
  1752	# substring-vs-exact class, for the fourth time in this plan.
  1753	_ENFORCEMENT_VOCAB = (
  1754	    "enforced today",
  1755	    "is enforced",
  1756	    "are enforced",
  1757	    "enforced by",
  1758	    "enforced at",
  1759	    "every tag publish",
  1760	    "recorded by",
  1761	    "is verified",
  1762	    "are verified",
  1763	    "verifies",
  1764	    "must satisfy",
  1765	    "guarantees",
  1766	    "is guaranteed",
  1767	    "are guaranteed",
  1768	    "asserted by",
  1769	    "checked by",
  1770	    "gated by",
  1771	    "fails closed",
  1772	    "fail closes",
  1773	    "asserts",
  1774	    "ensures",
  1775	    "is signed",
  1776	    "are signed",
  1777	)
